  "id": "chunk_14",
  "chunk": "that unconsciously shape our perception of their capabilities and lead us to\noverestimate their intelligence.\n2.2 Metacognition in LLMs\nWhile metacognition has been extensively studied in human psychology [10,\n29], relatively little work addresses metacognitive capabilities in AI systems.\nSome researchers have explored confidence calibration in neural networks [11]\nand uncertainty estimation [17], but these approaches differ fundamentally from\nhuman metacognition’s adaptive, reflective qualities.\nMitchell [23, 24] argues that current AI systems lack the self-monitoring\nnecessary to recognize when they are “stuck” and need to change strategies—a\n4\nlimitation that may be particularly evident in open-ended problem-solving con-\ntexts likeZork. Recent work by Yin et al. [37] on LLM self-evaluation found\nthat models can assess their own outputs but also highlight that there is a\nconsiderable gap between the capabilities of the models and human profiency.",
